  "notes": "An older godan verb meaning \"to fear.\" In modern Japanese, the ichidan form {恐|おそ}れる is far more common. The godan form {恐|おそ}る survives mainly in set expressions.\n\nTRANSITIVITY:\n- Type: {自動詞|じどうし} (intransitive)\n- No standard transitive pair\n- Pattern: {恐|おそ}る\n\nFORMS:\n- {恐|おそ}る: godan form (archaic/literary)\n- {恐|おそ}れる: ichidan form (standard modern usage)\n\nSET EXPRESSIONS:\n- {恐|おそ}れ{入|い}ります: I'm sorry to trouble you (very polite, used in business/service)\n- {恐|おそ}る{恐|おそ}る: timidly, gingerly (reduplication)\n- {恐|おそ}らく: probably, perhaps (adverb derived from this verb)\n\nCOMMON PATTERNS (using {恐|おそ}れる):\n- {失敗|しっぱい}を{恐|おそ}れる: to fear failure\n- {恐|おそ}れることはない: there's nothing to fear\n\nRELATED WORDS:\n- {恐|おそ}れ: fear (noun)\n- {恐|おそ}ろしい: frightening, terrible (adjective)\n- {怖|こわ}い: scary (more colloquial)",
